<figure id="A1.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span><span id="A1.T11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of Inference Temperature on Standard RAG(<span id="A1.T11.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ELI5</span> - <span id="A1.T11.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen-2.5-3B</span>).<span id="A1.T11.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
We vary only the decoding temperature <math id="A1.T11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at inference to study its effect on consistency and accuracy. Moderate temperature (<math id="A1.T11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>) improves LLM agreement and lexical consistency compared to deterministic decoding (<math id="A1.T11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>), while preserving accuracy. However, higher temperatures (<math id="A1.T11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) degrade both consistency and accuracy, with outputs at <math id="A1.T11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2.0</annotation></semantics></math> nearly collapsing.</span></span></figcaption>
